      "id": "m7dfqlh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Photosynthesis"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "psa1hl7",
      "content": "Photosynthesis is the process by which plants prepare food using sunlight, carbon dioxide and water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wo13zn1",
      "content": "Equation: 6CO2 + 6H2O → C6H12O6 + 6O2"
    },
    {
      "id": "5tle9g9",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Plant Nutrition"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Xylem Tissue",
        "Phloem Tissue"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Primary Function",
          "Transports water and dissolved minerals",
          "Transports food and organic nutrients"
        ],
        [
          "Direction of Transport",
          "Unidirectional (Root to aerial parts)",
          "Bidirectional (Leaves to all storage organs)"
        ],
        [
          "Living Status",
          "Mostly dead cells at maturity (except Xylem Parenchyma)",
          "Mostly living cells (except Phloem Fibres)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "df0m1mp"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b8dwtbu",
      "content": "• Autotrophic nutrition – plants make their own food."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gezb2fm",
      "content": "• Parasitic plants – obtain nutrients from host (e.g., Cuscuta)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l4v6pqr",
      "content": "• Insectivorous plants – trap insects for nutrients (e.g., pitcher plant)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t8z1zvw",
      "content": "• Symbiotic plants – mutual relationship with other organisms (e.g., lichens)."
    },
